<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vatican Radio</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1931">1931</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vatican Radio is the broadcasting service of the Holy See, set up by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guglielmo-marconi">Guglielmo Marconi</a> and inaugurated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-xi">Pope Pius XI</a> on 12 February 1931. Run for most of its history by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a>, it broadcasts in dozens of languages worldwide. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> it carried news and papal messages to listeners in occupied and communist countries.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Vatican Radio began broadcasting on 12 February 1931, with the callsign HVJ on two shortwave frequencies at 10 kilowatts, with a message from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-xi">Pope Pius XI</a>, Omni creaturae. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guglielmo-marconi">Guglielmo Marconi</a> and Cardinal Eugenio Pacelli, the future Pope Pius XII, were present. Its first director was the physicist Giuseppe Gianfranceschi. From the start it was maintained by the Jesuit Order, and in 1936 the International Telecommunication Union recognised it as a special case, authorising broadcasts without geographical limits.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1933 a microwave link joined the Vatican Palace and the papal summer residence at Castel Gandolfo, and in 1937 a 25 kW Telefunken transmitter and two directional antennas were added. After a December 1939 report by Cardinal August Hlond on the oppression of the Church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, Pius XII decided to use the radio to inform listeners of the Church's condition there. During <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> its news broadcasts were banned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, as all foreign broadcasts were, and it operated in four languages. Jacques Adler, who examined transcripts of wartime broadcasts, argues that they exposed Nazi persecution of the Church and opposed collaboration with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazism">Nazism</a>.</p><p>By 1948 the service had expanded to 18 languages. It was one of the 23 founding broadcasters of the European Broadcasting Union in 1950. A new broadcasting centre opened in 1957 on a 400-hectare site at Santa Maria di Galeria, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> had granted extraterritorial status in 1952. The station also made experimental television broadcasts in the 1930s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The station's interval signal, Christus Vincit, became a well-known sound on shortwave radio. In the 21st century it moved to digital transmission, podcasts and online channels. On 27 June 2015 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-francis">Pope Francis</a> established a Secretariat for Communications in the Roman Curia, which absorbed Vatican Radio on 1 January 2017, ending 85 years of independent operation. On 24 March 2017 it made its last English-language shortwave transmission to Asia, after 59 years of service.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vatican Radio” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
